ue problem\n$y^{″} = xy + y^{′}, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) = 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7aa5350-6bfc-5057-ad4a-3bd97a31da5e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Series Solution to a Second-Order Initial Value Problem About a Non-Zero Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0335c35-4d56-5b42-8d20-e723ed9aac47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = 1 + 2(x - π) + \\frac{1}{4}(x - π)^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}(x - π)^{3} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146793} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a79d85-f3cf-58ac-934b-bba8f8d8d4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = 1 + 2(x - π) - \\frac{1}{2}(x - π)^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}(x - π)^{3} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146794} {:answer/id #uuid "f53f296e-610f-5d27-a224-f5775fc383d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = 1 + 2(x - π) + \\frac{1}{4}(x - π)^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}(x - π)^{3} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146795} {:answer/id #uuid "0973a69a-7537-554e-a37f-9fbe0d157aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = 1 + 2(x - π) - \\frac{1}{2}(x - π)^{2} + \\frac{1}{6}(x - π)^{3} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146796} {:answer/id #uuid "2f6192fc-b9d4-5922-9571-902fc8a95c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = 1 + 2(x - π) - \\frac{1}{2}(x - π)^{2} - \\frac{1}{12}(x - π)^{3} + ⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146797}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d21d34ea-7104-5faf-a5e7-8f8ad9633b9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146792}] :question/id #uuid "4b0a8f90-3a88-582b-9f4a-0fbf1daa3eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-64561" :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x = π$, in ascending powers of $(x - π)$ up to and including the term in $(x - π)^{3}$, of the initial value problem\n$y^{″} = y\\cos x, y(π) = 1, y^{′}(π) = 2$."}] [{: